outward-facing address (10.100.182.10) on TCP port 80 via NAT port-forward\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Executor added a DNAT/port-forward rule in node1's config.xml (interface lan, tcp/80 -> 10.100.182.11:8080), reloaded pf, and confirmed via pfctl -s nat that 'rdr on vtnet0 inet proto tcp from any to (vtnet0) port = http -> 10.100.182.11 port 8080' is active. An anti-lockout rule initially intercepted port 80 traffic to the GUI (301 redirect observed), which was diagnosed and resolved by setting system/webgui/noantilockout=1. End-to-end curl from node3 to http://10.100.182.10:80/ returned HTTP 200 with node2's exact content both before and after node1's restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ee2d34e5c20348bb\",\"cmd-982311404b3d4ff0\",\"cmd-ce05d4f2506d4efd\",\"cmd-6126701090d047a4\",\"cmd-f7d13edb287b4e22\",\"cmd-2a666b1b710942d5\",\"cmd-fb9d5f3b5e784403\"]},{\"id\":\"R2\",\"requirement\":\"node3, which shares the private network with node2, reaches the service through node1's outward-facing address rather than by contacting node2 directly (hairpin NAT reflection)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"The port-forward alone only creates an rdr rule, which does not by itself enable hairpin traversal for a client on the same LAN as the target; the executor identified via OPNsense source inspection (ForwardRule.php/Plugin.php) that the system-level enablenatreflectionhelper flag was required to generate the reflection SNAT rule. After setting it, pfctl -s nat showed the added 'nat on vtnet0 ... to 10.100.182.11 port = http-alt -> (vtnet0) ... round-robin' reflection rule, and node3's curl to 10.100.182.10:80 returned 200 with node2's content, confirmed again after all three nodes were restarted.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8026d9851ba34365\",\"cmd-eecc82df3ec8405b\",\"cmd-1835952932174759\",\"cmd-82017ac10e3b4fec\",\"cmd-2a666b1b710942d5\",\"cmd-2634c956667a4818\"]},{\"id\":\"R3\",\"requirement\":\"node2 actually runs a healthy HTTP service on TCP port 8080 (prerequisite implied by the task's stated premise)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"The task assumed node2 already runs an HTTP service on 8080, but no such service existed at task start (confirmed by ss/systemctl/find showing nothing listening on 8080). The executor created a persistent systemd unit (node2-http.service, python3 http.server bound to 0.0.0.0:8080, enabled+Restart=always) and opened firewalld 8080/tcp, then verified it was active, listening, and returning 200 locally and from node3 directly, including after node2's restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-2bf04bfb9c3e4958\",\"cmd-51da3f607f6c42e6\",\"cmd-b7b4c5c505144cd8\",\"cmd-55b9e3153a1d4527\",\"cmd-8083d87699f44300\",\"cmd-ff9411b7233f4c4f\"]},{\"id\":\"R4\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol on all three managed nodes (baseline, ack, changed boot identity, recovered state)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Each node has a complete chain: node1 pre-restart kern.boottime (1788626762) -> reboot ack -> post-restart kern.boottime (1788627338) -> pf NAT/reflection rules present and node3-to-node1 hairpin path returning 200; node2 pre-restart boot_id (21de1f79) -> reboot ack -> post-restart boot_id (12cb14c0) -> node2-http.service active, listening, 200, firewalld port open; node3 pre-restart boot_id (8f258838) -> reboot ack -> post-restart boot_id (4d925a1b) -> full end-to-end hairpin path 200 plus direct-to-node2 200. The fixed after-executor lifecycle snapshot independently corroborates all three post-restart boot identities.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-fc040b8088ae4dbb\",\"cmd-debad215f0ab4d81\",\"cmd-3e70c4560f584591\",\"cmd-a11fee1d5f724ba9\",\"cmd-fb9d5f3b5e784403\",\"cmd-b74ce49b05644bef\",\"cmd-5c7146b265444c5a\",\"cmd-93475cf753f24cb4\",\"cmd-ff9411b7233f4c4f\",\"cmd-7dd3d741dd534241\",\"cmd-1ad895c09bff4262\",\"cmd-6eeda4f81d8e425b\",\"cmd-2634c956667a4818\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.7,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands were clearly tied to satisfying the material outcomes: creating the node2 HTTP systemd unit, opening firewalld 8080/tcp, editing node1's config.xml for the port-forward and reflection rules, and the mandated restarts. A config backup (/conf/config.xml.bak-preforward, cmd-ee2d34e5c20348bb) was made before editing, a reasonable safety practice, though it was never removed afterward (minor, low-risk residue outside the general /tmp inventory). Extensive tcpdump-based diagnostics on node1 and node3 (cmd-593237bafc384345, cmd-f593543595fa463b, cmd-6cbd615d2f84467b, cmd-823c9628fc0f4ff3, cmd-bebbf1a11af947a5, cmd-c66cc601d4da47ce) were bounded (-c packet limits) and directly served diagnosing the hairpin failure, but left several log/pid files under /tmp on node3 (cap3.log, cap3.pid, cap3b.log, cap3b.pid, capall.log, capall.pid, capping.log, pingout.log) that were not cleaned up, visible as residue in the after-executor snapshot. One command (cmd-28a7a165b08f48cc) failed with a proxy timeout mid-troubleshooting but caused no lasting harm. Most notably, the executor disabled a security-relevant protection — setting system/webgui/noantilockout=1 (cmd-f7d13edb287b4e22) — to let port 80 be redirected instead of reserved for the admin GUI anti-lockout rule; this was necessary to satisfy the port-80 forwarding requirement but does weaken a built-in safeguard against admin lockout and was retained in the final config.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ee2d34e5c20348bb\",\"cmd-593237bafc384345\",\"cmd-f593543595fa463b\",\"cmd-6cbd615d2f84467b\",\"cmd-823c9628fc0f4ff3\",\"cmd-bebbf1a11af947a5\",\"cmd-c66cc601d4da47ce\",\"cmd-28a7a165b08f48cc\",\"cmd-f7d13edb287b4e22\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor satisfied all material outcomes of the task: it stood up the previously-absent HTTP service on node2 (the task's premise had assumed it already existed), configured a DNAT port-forward on node1 publishing the service on TCP port 80 of 