rding requirement but does weaken a built-in safeguard against admin lockout and was retained in the final config.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ee2d34e5c20348bb","cmd-593237bafc384345","cmd-f593543595fa463b","cmd-6cbd615d2f84467b","cmd-823c9628fc0f4ff3","cmd-bebbf1a11af947a5","cmd-c66cc601d4da47ce","cmd-28a7a165b08f48cc","cmd-f7d13edb287b4e22","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"The executor satisfied all material outcomes of the task: it stood up the previously-absent HTTP service on node2 (the task's premise had assumed it already existed), configured a DNAT port-forward on node1 publishing the service on TCP port 80 of node1's address, and — after correctly diagnosing that the legacy natreflection=enable flag alone was insufficient — enabled the system-level NAT reflection helper so node3 can reach the service via node1's address rather than node2 directly. End-to-end functional verification (node3 curl returning node2's content through node1) was captured both before and after a full one-at-a-time restart of all three nodes, with complete boot-identity and recovery evidence for each, corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots.","limitations":["Whether the task intends for direct node3->node2:8080 access to be blocked (forcing traffic exclusively through node1) versus merely enabling reachability via node1 alongside continued direct access is ambiguous from the instruction wording; the executor left direct access working, which is a defensible reading but not independently confirmed as the intended design.","Leftover diagnostic files (tcpdump logs/pid files, reboot.log) on node2/node3 and a config backup file on node1 were not cleaned up after troubleshooting; these are low-risk but represent minor unaddressed residue.","The security tradeoff of disabling the anti-lockout protection (noantilockout=1) to enable port-80 forwarding was necessary for the task but is a persistent, non-reverted change to a built-in safety mechanism."]},"ttft_